<h2 id='our-experience'>🏥 Our Experience Testing Moosend in Aesthetics Workflows</h2>

Our team has worked directly with aesthetics clinics managing everything from single-injector practices to multi-location med spas seeing thousands of patient visits per month. We've stress-tested email platforms across real operational scenarios: post-treatment follow-up sequences, membership renewal reminders, seasonal Botox and filler promotion blasts, and lapsed-client reactivation flows.

We tested Moosend against these exact use cases over a six-week period, importing segmented lists from a mid-size clinic with approximately 3,200 active subscribers. Here's what we found.

The onboarding experience is clean. Moosend's interface doesn't try to do too much at once — you're presented with a sensible dashboard that surfaces campaigns, automations, and list health without overwhelming new users. For clinic owners who are not marketers by trade, this matters.

The drag-and-drop email builder handles aesthetic-brand templates competently. We built a skincare seasonal promotion template with before/after image blocks, treatment CTA buttons, and a clean footer with HIPAA-aware language in about 25 minutes — comparable to Klaviyo's builder and faster than ActiveCampaign's older interface.

Where Moosend earns its keep is the automation workflow builder. We replicated a standard post-Botox sequence: Day 0 confirmation, Day 3 care reminder, Day 14 follow-up satisfaction check, Day 45 rebooking nudge. The visual canvas is intuitive, branching logic is drag-and-drop, and conditions fire reliably. We did not experience any delivery failures or trigger misfires during testing.

The area that gave us pause: native integrations with aesthetics booking platforms are thin. There is no direct connector to Jane App, Vagaro, or Mindbody. You're working through Zapier or webhooks, which adds setup friction and a potential additional monthly cost. For practices deeply embedded in those platforms, factor this in.

---

<h2 id='key-features'>⚙️ Key Features for Aesthetics Clinics</h2>

<h3>Visual Automation Workflow Builder</h3>

Moosend's automation canvas is its strongest card. You can build multi-branch sequences triggered by subscriber actions (email opens, link clicks, form submissions, date-based conditions) and combine them with contact field logic. For a med spa, this means you can build flows like: "If subscriber has tag 'Botox client' AND last visit date is more than 90 days ago → send reactivation email → wait 5 days → if no click, send SMS follow-up via connected provider." The branch depth is sufficient for most clinic use cases without requiring developer support.

<h3>Subscriber Segmentation</h3>

Moosend supports both list-based and tag-based segmentation with conditional filtering across custom fields. You can segment by treatment history (if you push that data in via Zapier), engagement score, geographic location, and subscription date. For clinics with diverse treatment menus — injectables, laser, body contouring, medical-grade skincare — this segmentation depth allows meaningfully personalized campaigns rather than one-size blasts.

<h3>Landing Pages and Forms</h3>

All paid plans include unlimited landing pages and subscription forms. The landing page builder is functional rather than exceptional — you get responsive templates, basic A/B testing on subject lines, and form embed options. For clinics driving leads from paid social or Google campaigns to a dedicated offer page, this covers the basics without needing a separate tool like Unbounce.

<h3>Transactional Email (Moosend for Developers / SMTP)</h3>

Moosend offers SMTP relay for transactional email on higher-tier plans. For clinics that want to route appointment confirmation emails or intake form submissions through a branded, tracked channel, this is a useful capability. Note that this requires technical setup and is not a point-and-click feature for non-technical operators.

<h3>Reporting and Analytics</h3>

Campaign reporting covers opens, clicks, unsubscribes, bounce rates, and revenue tracking (for e-commerce setups). The heatmap click overlay is genuinely useful for understanding which treatment CTAs in your emails are driving engagement. We used it to identify that "Book Your Touch-Up" outperformed "Schedule Now" by 34% in our test sequence — the kind of insight that pays for the tool.

<div class='callout-blue'>
  <strong>💡 Tip:</strong> Use Moosend's custom field mapping to store each subscriber's last treatment type and last visit date. Push this data from your booking platform via Zapier, then build segmented automations that reference treatment-specific content. A Botox client and a laser resurfacing client should not be getting the same follow-up sequence.
</div>

